{"w":2400,"h":3106,"lines":[{"t":"Test 1","w":0.058270676930745446,"h":0.014613500964772097,"c":1,"x":0.14473684189490293,"y":0.061046511803616865},{"x":0.14661654134990035,"y":0.51162790703132999,"t":"F","w":0.015037593841552732,"c":1,"h":0.014534883321060899},{"h":0.023280122244810908,"t":"if allowed to fester.' For Eastwood, the central feature of boredom is a failure to","y":0.10530644100409192,"c":1,"x":0.18970060280541975,"w":0.72963612874348949},{"x":0.18972880253271532,"y":0.1247840234659332,"w":0.74831451416015626,"t":"put our 'attention system' into gear. This causes an inability to focus on anything,","h":0.021888428323896814,"c":1},{"y":0.14384206680644374,"x":0.18973964293490914,"t":"which makes time seem to go painfully slowly. What's more, your efforts to improve","w":0.77088307698567715,"h":0.021650357317018809,"c":1},{"x":0.18971176444315196,"y":0.16248623792334449,"w":0.7296010335286458,"h":0.022598779976790162,"c":1,"t":"the situation can end up making you feel worse. 'People try to connect with the"},{"h":0.024316534716623983,"c":1,"t":"world and if they are not successful there's that frustration and irritability,' he says.","x":0.18968564945124855,"y":0.18030337264373186,"w":0.7615488688151042},{"c":1,"t":"Perhaps most worryingly, says Eastwood, repeatedly failing to engage attention","h":0.023834896640323122,"w":0.73528172810872405,"x":0.1915780277679508,"y":0.20025426163139715},{"c":1,"x":0.19157587501600404,"t":"can lead to a state where we don't know what to do any more, and no longer care.","w":0.76156814575195309,"h":0.023942644029606219,"y":0.2198536430624709},{"h":0.021951037994600853,"c":1,"t":"Eastwood's team is now trying to explore why the attention system fails. It's early","x":0.19162436543349542,"y":0.24964607883414258,"w":0.74834966023763028},{"y":0.26838861099598321,"h":0.021801800552829986,"c":1,"t":"days but they think that at least some of it comes down to personality. Boredom","x":0.19159329906791964,"w":0.73522430419921869},{"y":0.28786727368964593,"x":0.19161145524888032,"w":0.7351793924967448,"c":1,"h":0.020356408565641582,"t":"proneness has been linked with a variety of traits. People who are motivated by"},{"w":0.77478154500325525,"t":"pleasure seem to suffer particularly badly. Other personality traits, such as curiosity,","c":1,"y":0.30501250737037522,"x":0.19151986700605639,"h":0.025356219034385341},{"t":"are associated with a high boredom threshold. More evidence that boredom has","c":1,"h":0.024228800364025749,"x":0.19154442872186336,"y":0.32423337662227092,"w":0.74282430013020828},{"h":0.019302768547613258,"x":0.19163975011464809,"y":0.34584572143413173,"t":"detrimental effects comes from studies of people who are more or less prone to","c":1,"w":0.73514322916666663},{"y":0.36429581738371153,"t":"boredom. It seems those who bore easily face poorer prospects in education,","x":0.19164341800161441,"c":1,"h":0.020147686532752007,"w":0.71819753011067711},{"c":1,"y":0.38396526741779935,"t":"their career and even life in general. But of course, boredom itself cannot kill -","h":0.021435523294128234,"w":0.72579193115234375,"x":0.19163027337941838},{"h":0.021377715769692163,"x":0.19348409306376746,"c":1,"y":0.40083608026715245,"w":0.73709599812825521,"t":"it's the things we do to deal with it that may put us in danger. What can we do to"},{"c":1,"x":0.19343405553842585,"w":0.77289866129557305,"t":"alleviate it before it comes to that? Goetz's group has one suggestion. Working with","h":0.023571876579918327,"y":0.41955862537998068},{"y":0.43762295378668048,"c":1,"t":"teenagers, they found that those who 'approach' a boring situation - in other words,","x":0.19156403928407209,"w":0.77657602945963555,"h":0.025257773807719652},{"w":0.75773976643880214,"h":0.021487587893769144,"y":0.4591834800367377,"c":1,"x":0.19349094575150275,"t":"see that it's boring and get stuck in anyway - report less boredom than those who"},{"w":0.59989802042643237,"t":"try to avoid it by using snacks, TV or social media for distraction.","x":0.19158140067899335,"y":0.47871743157273194,"h":0.023228701206460256,"c":1},{"t":"Psychologist Francoise Wemelsfelder speculates that our over-connected lifestyles","x":0.19342818581669546,"y":0.50581474253545688,"c":1,"w":0.76914972941080717,"h":0.024118780859500699},{"y":0.52570307910525804,"h":0.022429086128819442,"c":1,"w":0.77095575968424468,"t":"might even be a new source of boredom. 'In modern human society there is a lot of","x":0.19348027566560916},{"t":"overstimulation but still a lot of problems finding meaning,' she says. So instead of","x":0.19345560188339656,"y":0.54499665867966307,"h":0.023908190165345844,"c":1,"w":0.76162267049153654},{"t":"seeking yet more mental stimulation, perhaps we should leave our phones alone,","y":0.56451842770954119,"w":0.75591328938802094,"h":0.025106153715370727,"x":0.19345517613998481,"c":1},{"h":0.021857419477611439,"c":1,"x":0.1934484829945517,"y":0.58420490626276023,"t":"and use boredom to motivate us to engage with the world in a more","w":0.62625920613606767},{"w":0.15413533528645834,"x":0.19360902399535002,"h":0.017466194462330997,"t":"meaningful way.","c":1,"y":0.60753380563612625}]}